  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 135 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMonday through Friday:\n\nSynopsis: High pressure will shift toward Ontario & the Saint \nLawrence Valley Monday afternoon & evening, allowing southerly \nwinds & warm/moist advection to commence areawide. An upper \ntrough will concurrently advance toward the Lake Superior \nvicinity, with attendant surface cyclogenesis near Lake Winnipeg\nfurther reinforcing the southerly return flow & warm advection.\nThe developing surface low will pull a cold front into the \nwestern Great Lakes Monday night into Tuesday, with the boundary\nstalling near or south of the Wisconsin-Illinois border through\nthe mid-week time frame. Between the amplifying warm/moist \nadvection & encroaching surface front, additional periods of \nshowers and storms are thus expected from Monday night into at \nleast Tuesday. Whether or not precip chances will linger into \nWednesday will depend on the stalling location of the passing \nsurface front, with a number of different solutions apparent in \ncurrent forecast guidance. Some storms---particularly those \noccurring Monday night & Tuesday---could be stronger with \nlocally heavy rainfall. The next area of high pressure will \narrive by Thursday, bringing quieter and seasonable conditions \nfor the end of next week.\n\nMonday Evening & Night: Shower and storm chances increase \nareawide given an abundance of arriving large scale forcing. \nGiven the synoptics in play, confidence is high in the \noccurrence of widespread showers and storms, with the 50-80%+ \nprecip probabilities from current NBM guidance appearing more \nthan reasonable. What remains less certain is the hazards \npotential---particularly locally heavy rainfall & damaging wind \ngusts---that will accompany the expected storms. Materialization\nof hazards will reside in the placement & arrival timing of the\nunseasonably moist & modestly unstable air mass being ushered \nin by southerly return flow. Abundant deep layer shear on the \norder of 40+ kts will overlap with this air mass, supporting at \nleast some potential for organized storms capable of gusty \nwinds. How far this air mass gets & how quickly it does so will \ndepend on the relative strengths of departing high pressure & \nthe surface low forming near Lake Winnipeg. Any combination of \nstronger high pressure and/or weaker low pressure would result \nin a slower-arriving favorable air mass confined to more western\nparts of the area. Opposite combinations of high & low \nstrengths would conversely allow favorable thermodynamics to \narrive over more of southern Wisconsin in shorter order. Air \nmass recovery & its relation to evolving surface features will \nthus be the primary trend to monitor into this weekend. Current \nforecast thinking favors that of a more western solution, with \nthe understanding that additional shifts are possible as \nadditional forecast guidance arrives through the weekend. Will \ncontinue to provide updates in coming forecasts. \n\nTuesday: Depending on where the primary cold front ends up in \nthe wake of Monday night precip, could see more shower & storm \ndevelopment with additional locally heavy rainfall potential. \nHave trended likely (~60+%) precip probabilities toward the \ncurrently- favored position of the cold frontal boundary, which \nwould place areas near/south of I-94 & US-18 in line for the \nbest chances of additional storm development. Anticipate \nadditional shifts in preferred corridors for storm \nredevelopment, with trends being monitored over coming updates.\n\nQuigley",
